Add basic_stacktrace::from_exception(const std::exception_ptr&)

Boost.Stacktrace can only hand back the trace of the exception *currently
being handled* (from_current_exception). Its POSIX implementation already
works in terms of an exception_ptr internally, though: the trace is dumped
into the tail of the exception allocation and a pointer to it is parked in
the spare `reserve` field of the Itanium ABI header, which is reached by
dereferencing the exception_ptr and stepping back a fixed offset. All
from_current_exception adds on top is a call to std::current_exception().

So this splits that last step out into a new exported entry point taking any
exception_ptr, and reimplements current_exception_stacktrace() on top of it.
That makes traces reachable for stored or cross-thread exceptions, which is
what P2370's exception_ptr-shaped API would want anyway.

ELF + GCC-compatible compilers only; the MSVC/MinGW implementation keys its
side table on a Windows exception record rather than the exception object,
and is left untouched (from_exception is simply not declared there).

Not submitted upstream.

